# Committed KSP game state used as automated-test input.
#
# WHY. These files are authored by KSP on Windows and several gates compare them
# BYTE-FOR-BYTE against freshly generated output: `build_career_pad_craft.py
# --check`, `build_gs1_craft.py` / `build_dd1_craft.py --check`, `FixtureDriftTests`
# (which re-runs the career-pad splice and asserts byte-identity with the committed
# save), and `SharedShipsManifestTests` (which hashes the whole fixture tree). A
# contributor whose git has `core.autocrlf=true` would otherwise get CRLF
# working-tree copies of the ~295,000 lines of `.sfs` these trees hold, and every
# one of those gates would then compare CRLF-on-disk against LF-generated bytes and
# red for a reason that has nothing to do with the change in front of them.
#
# `-text` disables end-of-line conversion in both directions, so what is committed
# is what lands in the working tree on every platform.
#
# NOT `-diff`, deliberately. Marking these as undiffable would shrink PR diffs -
# which is tempting, given a fixture PR can run six figures of lines - but it would
# also hide every fixture change from review, and a fixture edit is exactly the kind
# of change that needs reading (a missing `ParsekScenario` node in `career-pad-craft`
# once cost a full flight to diagnose). Size is addressed by not committing
# redundant data, not by making the data unreadable. Git still auto-detects the
# genuinely binary sidecars (`.prec`, `.pann`, `<id>_ghost.craft`,
# `<id>_vessel.craft` - all NUL-bearing) and diffs those as binary on its own, while
# the text ones (`.sfs`, the `ships/*.craft` library, `.prec.txt`) stay readable.
#
# The second pattern is forward-looking: `Source/Parsek.Tests/Fixtures/` holds only
# `KspLog/*.log` today, the DefaultCareer save tree having been deleted. It is kept
# so a future KSP-authored fixture placed there is covered from the first commit
# rather than after someone's gate reds.
harness/fixtures/**    -text
Source/Parsek.Tests/Fixtures/**  -text
